.TH CYAML_SERIALIZE_FLOW 3 "2026-09-06" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
cyaml_serialize_flow \- serialize a DOM tree to flow-style YAML
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cyaml.h>

char *cyaml_serialize_flow(cyaml node);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Produce a compact, flow-style YAML text of the DOM subtree.  A list
becomes [a, b, c].  A dictionary becomes {key: value, ...}.  A scalar
obeys the same quoting rules as in cyaml_serialize().  The output is
normally one line.

The members of a dictionary go out in insertion order, and a float
goes out with a '.' in its mantissa, exactly as cyaml_serialize(3)
describes.

The rule for a tag is the same as in cyaml_serialize().  The function
writes a custom tag verbatim as "!<...>".  It never writes a
core-schema tag, because a node only ever carries the core-schema tag
of its own type.  A byte that the URI characters of YAML 1.2 do not
allow literally inside "!<...>" becomes a percent escape here too.  A second parse of the tag therefore gives
the identical string.  See cyaml_serialize(3).

The rule for a dictionary key is also the same.  A key that is exactly
the canonical text of an integer or a float key goes out with no
quotes, and every key that a parse would read as another type or as
another text goes out double-quoted.  A key whose rendered text is
longer than 1024 bytes goes out as an explicit "? key: value" entry of
the flow mapping, for the reason that cyaml_serialize(3) gives.  The
rule for the decimal point of a float and the locale is the same as
there too.

The string that this function gives comes from the allocator of the
node.  Free it with cyaml_serialize_free() when the tree used the
default allocator.  Free it with cyaml_serialize_free_mp() when the
tree used a custom allocator, and give that same allocator.
cyaml_serialize_free() hands the buffer to plain free(), and plain
free() never allocated a buffer that a custom allocator gave.

node obeys the same 500-level nesting limit as in cyaml_serialize(3).
This function also walks the tree with an explicit worklist, not by
recursion, for the same reason.  See the DESCRIPTION of that page.

A NULL node serializes in the same way as a CYAML_NULL node ("~").
This is a successful, non-NULL return, and not a signal of failure.
cyaml_clone(3) differs here, because it returns NULL for a NULL node.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
A null-terminated string on the heap on success.  The function returns
NULL when it runs out of memory, and also when node is nested more than
500 levels deep.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cyaml_dictionary_first (3),
.BR cyaml_serialize (3),
.BR cyaml_serialize_stream (3),
.BR cyaml_serialize_free (3),
.BR cyaml_serialize_free_mp (3)
